  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nNotably drier conditions are in place across the region today, \nleading to a break in our recent pattern of afternoon and evening \nconvection. ACARS soundings from KDEN depict the moisture-starved \nprofile well, and although a minority of CAMs insist on producing a \nfew weak high-based showers this evening, this seems rather unlikely \nwith water vapor imagery showing a pronounced dry slot over southern \nColorado progressing slowly northward. Even if mid-level moisture is \nsufficient, high bases and dry near-surface conditions should \nrestrict any such activity to virga.\n\nChanges into Friday will be subtle. Around 0.5C of warming at 700mb, \nalong with a less defined Denver cyclone signature and mostly sunny \nconditions through mid afternoon will allow for a few degrees of \nwarming, pushing highs into the mid 90's for the lower elevations. A \nminor southerly shift in flow aloft will be enough to draw up some \nmodest moisture, with precipitation chances creeping back into the \nforecast. These will generally be confined to the high country, \nparticularly along and south of I-70 where southerly flow is most \norographically favorable, though a few late afternoon showers could \ndrift into the adjacent plains while weakening. There won't be much \nforcing to sustain activity for very long.\n\nThe southerly moisture stream will continue into Saturday as we \nremain on the western periphery of a slowly expanding ridge to our \neast, resulting in largely isolated high-based showers and a little \nbetter coverage in the mountains. Come Sunday the ridge looks to \nflatten slightly, with a corresponding turn to southeasterly flow\naloft, helping to work in just enough low-level moisture to allow\nfor moderately unstable conditions and a return to more scattered\nafternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms both Sunday and \nMonday, including for the plains and urban corridor. There will be\nlittle effect on temperatures however, with low pressure \nremaining well to our northwest and subsident flow east of the \nFront Range keeping temperatures 5-10 degrees above normal for the\ndate.\n\nPast Monday, there's increased variability in ensemble members and \nthus more uncertainty in the forecast, although the overall picture \ndoesn't appear drastically different to the near term. Broadly, high \npressure looks to prevail over the central US plains, with troughing \nto our west maintaining some form of SW to W flow aloft. Above \naverage temperatures remain favored to persist through much of the \nweek independent of the exact strength/placement of the ridge, and \nthe flow pattern would support at least isolated to scattered \nshower/storm coverage most days for the plains and mountains \nrespectively, albeit with uncertainty as far as which days would \nbring the higher coverage.",
